# Expectation record for 03_initializers/005_designated_struct.c
#
# Designated struct member initializers: declaration order, reverse order, omitted
# members, nested-member designators, designator-then-positional continuation, and
# designators descending into an array member.
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 005_designated_struct
area               = 03_initializers
description        = Designated struct member initializers -- in declaration order, in reverse member order, with members omitted, with nested-member designators, with designator-then-positional continuation exercising the C11 current-object rule, and with designators descending into an array member -- for static and automatic storage duration, every member read back individually
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined and no unspecified behaviour.

THERE IS NO READ OF UNINITIALIZED STORAGE, even though several initializers omit
members. Every object here that is printed has an initializer, and C11 6.7.9p19
and p21 require members not explicitly initialized to be initialized implicitly
as an object with static storage duration would be -- that is, to zero for the int
members used here. This covers g_omitted's nested in.p and in.q, g_nested_mem's
in.p and trailer, g_then_pos's tag, g_arr_elem's unnamed array elements and
trailer, l_omitted's tag, in.q and trailer, and l_arr_elem's unnamed array
elements, tag and trailer.

NO INITIALIZER SUPPLIES A SECOND VALUE FOR ANY MEMBER. Each designated initializer
in this program names each member at most once, and where a designator is followed
by a positional initializer the positional one lands on the NEXT member rather
than on one already set. Specifically, in g_then_pos the initializer
{ .in = { 42, 43 }, 44 } sets in.p and in.q through the member designator, after
which C11 6.7.9p17 and p18 make the current object advance to the member following
in, so the bare 44 initializes trailer; tag is never named and is therefore
implicitly zero. There is consequently no overlapping or overriding initializer
anywhere in this program, and the result is fully determined by the standard.

No arithmetic is performed on any initialized value, so signed overflow is
impossible. The only run-time arithmetic is on the loop counter i, bounded i < 4
over the array member a[4], so every subscript is in bounds; no one-past-end
pointer is formed and none is dereferenced. That counter is the one object in the
file declared without an initializer, and it is assigned in each for statement's
initial clause before any read of it, so it too is never read uninitialized. The
array-member designators .a[0], .a[2] and .a[3] are all within the declared bound
of 4.

No shifts, no pointer casts, no aliasing -- each object is read only through its
own declared type. Nothing is modified twice between two sequence points. The
seven initialized structures are never modified after their initializers at all,
so every value printed is the value its initializer gave it; the only object that
is modified is the loop counter i, assigned in the initial clause of each of the
three for statements at 005_designated_struct.c lines 116, 121 and 138 and
incremented in each of their controlling expressions. Every one of those
assignments and increments is a full expression of its own, so each is separated
from every other access to i by a sequence point, and the bound i < 4 is a literal
equal to the extent of the array member it walks, so i neither overflows nor
leaves an array. Each printf call has at most one argument with a side effect, and
none has one.

NO PADDING BYTE IS READ OR COMPARED. Only named members are accessed; no struct is
printed as a whole and no object representation is inspected. Padding contents are
unspecified, so this is essential to the soundness of the byte-exact comparison.
All three struct types are composed exclusively of int members and an int array,
so on every supported target they have no internal padding in any case.

EVERY PARTIAL INITIALIZER LIST IN THIS PROGRAM IS INTRODUCED BY A DESIGNATOR, AND
THAT IS DELIBERATE. Under the mandated authoring gate, -Wextra enables
-Wmissing-field-initializers and -Werror promotes it to a hard error. Measurement
with the reference compiler established that in C this diagnostic does NOT fire for
a list whose members are named by designators, nor for the { 0 } idiom, but DOES
fire for a positional partial struct such as `struct outer v = { 1 };`. Omitting
members is therefore safe here precisely because no list omits a member without a
designator naming where the values it does supply belong.

The rule is stated that way rather than as "every initializer is designated",
because two spellings in this program are positional on purpose and the precise
rule is what makes both of them safe. Every nested brace list is written
POSITIONALLY and is COMPLETE -- { 2, 3 } and { 12, 13 } and { 42, 43 } fill both
members of struct inner, and { 62, 63, 64, 65 } fills all four elements of a[4] --
so nothing is omitted for the diagnostic to fire on. And g_then_pos is the
deliberate designator-to-positional continuation the program exists to test:
{ .in = { 42, 43 }, 44 } supplies its trailing value POSITIONALLY, continuing from
the member the designator selected, which is exactly the construct described above
and the one whose lowering a compiler can get wrong. It is a partial list -- tag is
never named -- and it draws no diagnostic because the designator .in establishes
where the continuation begins. What may not be done in this program is to convert
one of the DESIGNATED partial lists into a purely positional partial list. The
positional-partial-struct spelling is exercised nowhere in area 03, and that
exclusion together with its reason is recorded in 003_partial_zero_fill.expected,
which is the program whose subject is implicit zero fill.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
All printed values are int and are printed with %d only. The program contains no
long, size_t, ptrdiff_t, intptr_t, float, double or long double, so the measured
width difference between i686 (4-byte long and pointer) and the other three
targets (8-byte) cannot affect the output.

The only character data is the format-string literals, whose bytes printf copies
through unchanged. No plain-char value is read as a number, converted, compared or
formatted numerically, so the measured plain-char signedness difference (signed on
x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64) cannot arise.

No address or pointer value is printed, and no struct is printed as a whole, so
padding bytes -- whose contents are unspecified -- are never observed.

Two line groups carry disproportionate diagnostic value. g_then_pos must read
tag=0, in.p=42, in.q=43, trailer=44: a compiler that resets the current object to
the first member after a designator, or that fails to advance past the nested
struct, produces a visibly different four-line group. g_reordered and l_reordered
supply their designators OUT of member order -- g_reordered exactly reversed, and
l_reordered permuted differently so that the two are not the same case twice --
yet must produce ascending values, so a defect in designator resolution appears as
transposed values rather than as a crash.

Output is byte-identical on all four targets at all three optimization levels,
verified by measurement.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
